e W4-W5-W6 Chow. If I discard W6, I lose that possibility. So bad.\n\n- **Play F2**: If I discard F2, I lose the chance to form a Pung of South Wind (F2). Since the prevailing wind is South, having a Pung of South Wind would give me a Prevalent Wind score. So better to keep F2.\n\n- **Play B1**: Part of a potential B1-B2-B3 Chow. Discard it if I can get B2 and B3, but not sure.\n\n- **Play T2**: Part of a potential T1-T2-T3 Chow. Discard it if I can get T3, but not sure.\n\n- **Play W1**: Part of a potential Pung or pair. Discard only if necessary.\n\n- **Play T9**: Single tile, not part of any meld. Safe to discard.\n\n- **Play W4**: Part of the W4-W5-W6 Chow. Discard and lose that.\n\n- **Play T1**: Part of potential T1-T2-T3. Discard if I can get T3.\n\nSo the best options are to discard W2 or T9, as they are not part of any potential melds. Between them, which is better?\n\nIf I discard W2, I'm getting rid of a tile that's not useful. If I discard T9, same. But maybe T9 is more likely to be useful to others. For example, the player across has a meld of B7-B8-B9, so if they have B9 in their meld, but I discard T9, they can't use it. However, the next player has a T9 in their history (they played T9). So maybe T9 is less useful to others. Alternatively, if I discard T9, and someone has T7-T8, they could form a Chow with T7-T8-T9. But I have T6-T7-T8 in my meld, so others might not need T9. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, discarding W2 is safer. So I think the best move is to discard W2.\n\nAnother angle: the current hand has a possible Chow of W4-W5-W6. If I keep all three, that's a meld. So I need to keep W4, W5, W6. So don't discard any of them. So Play W5 and Play W6 are out.\n\nSo remaining options are Play W2, Play F2, Play B1, Play T2, Play W1, Play T9, Play W4 (but W4 is part of the Chow, so discard it?), Play T1.\n\nWait, W4 is part of the W4-W5-W6 Chow, so I shouldn't discard it. So Play W4 is not a good idea.\n\nSo the options are Play W2, Play F2, Play B1, 