ave one each. Not helpful. \n\nThe F1 and J1 are honor tiles. If I can form a pair with them. But again, one each. \n\nSo perhaps I need to look for possible melds. Let's see. \n\nThe W1x2 could be part of a pung if I get another W1. But that's risky because there are only two left. \n\nLooking at the W2 in my hand. If I play W2, maybe someone else will discard another W2, but there's only one left. \n\nAlternatively, looking at the W4. Since I have a meld [W4, W5, W6], maybe I can form another chow with W4? But that's already part of the existing chow. \n\nWait, maybe I can form a pung with F1 or J1. But I only have one each. \n\nHmm. Let me think about possible combinations. \n\nIf I can form a pung with W1, that would be three W1s. Then I have the two chows, and the remaining tiles would be W2, T1, B1, F1, J1. But that's five tiles. Not helpful. \n\nAlternatively, maybe forming a pair with F1 and J1. But no, they are different. \n\nWait, perhaps the T1 and B1 are terminals. If I can form a pair with them, but I need two of the same. \n\nWait, maybe I need to look for a way to form a pair and two more melds. \n\nLooking at the remaining tiles: W1x2, W2, W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nIf I can form a pair with W1, then I need two more melds. The remaining tiles would be W1, W2, W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nBut how to form two melds from that? \n\nAlternatively, maybe discard a tile to allow for a better wait. \n\nThe valid actions are Play W2, Play B1, Play W1, Play F1, Play J1, Play W4, Play T1. \n\nLet me consider each option. \n\nIf I play W2: Then my hand has W1x2, W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nIf I play B1: Then hand has W1x2, W2, W4, T1, F1, J1. \n\nIf I play W1: Then hand has W1, W2, W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nIf I play F1: Then hand has W1x2, W2, W4, T1, B1, J1. \n\nIf I play J1: Then hand has W1x2, W2, W4, T1, B1, F1. \n\nIf I play W4: Then hand has W1x2, W2, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nIf I play T1: Then hand has W1x2, W2, W4, B1, F1, J1. \n\nNow, which of these plays would leave me 