 honors. They might not be part of any chows. \n\nIf I discard T1, B1, F1, or J1, maybe that's better. \n\nFor example, if I discard T1, then my hand has W1x2, W2, W4, B1, F1, J1. \n\nBut still, how to form the necessary melds. \n\nWait, maybe the key is to form a pair and three pungs. \n\nFor example, if I can form a pair with F1 or J1, and then three pungs. \n\nBut I only have one of each. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the two W1s as a pair, and then the remaining tiles can form two pungs. \n\nBut I have W2, W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nNot seeing any pungs here. \n\nHmm. This is tricky. Maybe I need to look for a way to form a seven pairs hand. \n\nSeven pairs require seven pairs. Let's see. \n\nCurrent hand: two W1s (a pair), and the rest are single. \n\nIf I can form pairs with W2, W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. But each is only one tile. So unless I can draw another of each. \n\nBut that's unlikely. \n\nAlternatively, maybe discard a tile to allow for a better wait. \n\nFor example, if I discard W2, then my hand has W1x2, W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nIf I can form a pair with W1, then the remaining tiles would be W4, T1, B1, F1, J1. \n\nBut again, no melds. \n\nWait, maybe the best option is to discard W2, as it's a middle tile and might not be part of any useful chow. \n\nIf I discard W2, then maybe others will discard W1s or W3s, allowing me to form a pung or chow. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard W1, but that leaves me with one W1, which is part of a potential pung. \n\nBut since there are two W1s left in the wall, maybe keeping both is better. \n\nAlternatively, discard a tile like F1 or J1, which are honor tiles. \n\nIf I discard F1, then my hand has W1x2, W2, W4, T1, B1, J1. \n\nStill not helpful. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best move is to discard W2, as it's a middle tile and might not be needed. \n\nSo, the valid action would be Play W2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has two W1s and potential to form a pung if another W1 is drawn. Discarding W2, a middle tile with limited chow potential, allows focusing on completing a pung of W1s or other melds.\nReason: Discarding W2 preserves the two W1s for a possible pung and avoids blocking other players from completing their melds, while maintaining flexibility for higher-value patterns like a pung of W1s or terminal pairs.\nAnswer: Play W2"}
